/*!
    \page qtbridges-python-limitations.html
    \title Qt Bridges - Python API Limitations
    \brief QtBridge Python API Limitations

    This document outlines the current limitations and constraints of the
    Qt Bridges framework when integrating Python backends with QML frontends.

    \section1 1. No Constructor Arguments from QML

    \b {Limitation:} QML cannot pass constructor arguments when instantiating
    Python classes registered with \c {bridge_type()}. This is the default
    behavior for all QML object types, including those registered from C++ and
    Python.

    \b {Description:} QML always creates objects using their default
    (zero-argument) constructors. There is no mechanism in QML to pass
    arguments to a class constructor at instantiation time. Instead, properties
    are set after construction, either via QML property bindings or
    assignments.

    \b {Best Practice:}
    \list
        \li Use properties to configure your Python class from QML after construction.
        \li For initialization logic that must run after all properties are
            set, use the \c {@complete} decorator (which maps to QML's
            \c {Component.onCompleted} and Qt's
            \c {QQmlParserStatus::componentComplete()}).
    \endlist


    \section1 2. Type Hints Required for Proper Type Conversion

    \b {Limitation:} Python type hints are mandatory for correct type inference
    and conversion between Python and QML.

    \b {Description:} QtBridge relies on Python type annotations to determine
    how to convert data types between Python and QML. Without type hints, the
    framework cannot correctly identify:
    \list
        \li Property types (e.g., whether a property returns a list, dict, or custom object)
        \li Method parameter types
        \li Method return types
    \endlist

    \b {Impact:}
    \list
        \li Properties returning lists without type hints may not be correctly
            exposed as \c {QVariantList} in QML
        \li Methods without type hints may fail to convert arguments or return
            values properly
        \li Table (not implemented yet) and List models require properly typed
            \c {data()} methods
    \endlist

    \b {Best Practice:} Always use type hints for all public methods and
    properties that will be accessed from QML.

    \section1 3. No Custom Signal Declarations

    \b {Limitation:} QtBridge does not support manual signal declarations in
    user code.

    \b {Description:} Unlike standard PySide6, you cannot use \c {Signal()}
    objects to declare custom signals. QtBridge automatically generates change
    signals for properties, but these signals are only emitted when the
    property setter is called from QML. If you set the property from Python
    code, the change signal is \b {not} emitted and QML will not be notified of
    the change. To define custom signals, it is recommended to use PySide6.

    \b {Workaround:} If you need to notify QML of a property change, ensure the
    property is set from QML. For changes initiated in Python that must notify
    QML, consider using PySide6.

    \b {Example:}

    \code [python]
    from QtBridge import bridge_type, qtbridge

    class StatusModel:
        def __init__(self):
            self._status = "idle"

        @property
        def status(self) -> str:
            return self._status

        @status.setter
        def status(self, value: str):
            self._status = value

    @qtbridge(module="StatusModel")
    def main():
        bridge_type(StatusModel, uri="backend", version="1.0")
    \endcode

    \code [qml]
    import backend 1.0

    ApplicationWindow {
        StatusModel {
            id: statusModel

            // This will only be triggered if status is set from QML
            onStatusChanged: {
                console.log("Status changed to:", status)
            }
        }

        Button {
            text: "Set to Processing"
            onClicked: statusModel.status = "processing"
        }
    }
    \endcode

    \section1 4. Limited Post-Registration Updates for \c {bridge_instance}
              with Containers

    \b {Limitation:} Python containers (list, tuple) and numpy arrays exposed
    via \c {bridge_instance()} are wrapped at registration time and subsequent
    modifications are not automatically tracked.

    \b {Description:} When you pass a Python container and numpy arrays to \c
    {bridge_instance()}, QtBridge wraps it in a \c {QRangeModel} to make it
    accessible from QML. However, this is a snapshot at registration time.
    Modifications to the original Python container after calling \c
    {bridge_instance()} will not be reflected in QML. Additionally, any
    modifications done to this data from QML using QRangeModel methods will not
    update the original Python container.

    \b {Impact:}
    \list
        \li Adding, removing, or modifying items in the original Python
            container after registration has no effect on the QML model
        \li The QML model becomes "frozen" at the state when
            \c {bridge_instance()} was called
        \li Modifications from QML do not propagate back to the original Python
            container
    \endlist

    \b {Workaround:} Use a class with a \c {data()} method and model decorators
    (\c {@insert}, \c {@remove}, \c {@edit}) for dynamic models that need to be
    updated after registration.

    \section1 5. Limited Property Types

    \b {Limitation:} Not all Python types can be seamlessly exposed as QML
    properties.

    \b {Description:} While QtBridge handles common types well (int, float,
    str, bool, list, dict), complex custom types may not convert properly.
    Nested complex objects and certain Python-specific types may lose
    information when crossing the Python-QML boundary.

    \b {Supported Types:}
    \list
        \li Basic types: \c {int}, \c {float}, \c {str}, \c {bool}, \c {None}
        \li Collections: \c {list}, \c {tuple}
        \li Dataclasses with basic types
        \li Custom classes registered with \c {bridge_type()}
    \endlist

    \b {Workaround:}
    \list
        \li Use dataclasses for structured data
        \li Flatten complex nested structures
        \li Register custom classes explicitly with \c {bridge_type()} if they
            need to be used as types in QML
    \endlist

    \section1 6. No QML Attached Properties

    \b {Limitation:} Python classes cannot define QML attached properties.

    \b {Description:} QtBridge does not support creating attached properties
    (like \c {Layout.fillWidth} or \c {Keys.onPressed}) from Python. Attached
    properties are a QML-specific feature that requires special QML engine
    integration.

    \section1 7. Single Module Registration

    \b {Limitation:} Each \c {bridge_type()} class can only be registered to
    one QML module URI.

    \b {Description:} You cannot register the same Python class under multiple
    QML module URIs. Each class can only have one QML type registration.

    \b {Workaround:} Use inheritance or wrapper classes if you need similar
    types in different modules.

    \section1 8. No Q_INVOKABLE Equivalent

    \b {Limitation:} All public methods are automatically exposed to QML -
    there's no way to hide specific methods.

    \b {Description:} Unlike PySide6 where you can use \c {@Slot()} to
    explicitly mark methods as invokable, QtBridge automatically exposes all
    public methods (those not starting with \c {_}) to QML.

    \b {Impact:}
    \list
        \li Internal helper methods must use the \c {_} prefix to prevent QML
            access
        \li No fine-grained control over method visibility
    \endlist

    \b {Workaround:} Use Python naming conventions (\c {_method_name} for
    internal, \c {method_name} for public/QML-accessible).

    \section1 9. No Python-to-QML Signal Emission (basically related to the
              above Singal support issue)

    \b {Limitation:} Property change signals are only emitted when properties
    are set from QML, not from Python code.

    \b {Description:} QtBridge automatically generates \c
    {<propertyName>Changed} signals for all properties. However, these signals
    are \b {only emitted when the property is set from QML}. If you modify a
    property value from Python code (e.g., in response to a timer, network
    event, or other Python-side logic), the corresponding change signal will \b
    {not} be emitted, and QML will not be notified of the change.

    \b {Impact:}
    \list
        \li QML UI will not update automatically when properties change from
            Python side
        \li \b {onPropertyChanged} handlers in QML won't be triggered by
            Python-initiated changes
        \li Property bindings in QML won't re-evaluate when Python modifies the
            value
    \endlist

    \b {Workaround:} For Python-initiated updates that need to notify QML, you
    must use PySide6 directly

    \section1 10. QRangeModel Wrapper Limitations (for bridge_instance())

    \b {Limitation:} When Python lists, tuples, 2D arrays or NumPy arrays are
    exposed to QML via \c {bridge_instance()}, they are wrapped in a \c
    {QRangeModel}. This wrapper does not behave like a Python list or NumPy
    array and can only be manipulated using the \c {QRangeModel} API methods.

    \b {Description:} \c {QRangeModel} provides a QML-friendly table/list model
    interface that supports indexed access, inserting, removing, and editing
    rows and columns. However, once a Python container is wrapped, it no longer
    retains Python’s list/array semantics. QML code cannot use normal list
    operations such as \c {push()}, \c {append()}, direct indexing (\c
    {model[row]}), etc. Additionally, mutation support depends on the original
    container type:
    \list
        \li NumPy arrays and list-of-lists (2D arrays) support full mutation
           (\c {insertRows}, \c {removeRows}, \c {setData}).
        \li Plain Python lists and tuples do not support structural or item
            updates through \c {QRangeModel}. Only \c {data()} can be used reliably.
    \endlist

    \b {Impact:}
    \list
        \li Built-in methods for \c {list}, \c {tuple}, \c {2D arrays} and \c
            {NumPy} cannot be used.
        \li Indexing syntax such as \c {model[row]} or \c {model[row][column]}
            is not supported.
        \li The QML model becomes independent of the original Python list —
            changes are not synchronized back.
        \li Only NumPy array and 2D array structures behave like editable table
            models. Simple lists and tuples cannot be modified using \c {insertRows},
            \c {removeRows}, or \c {setData}.
        \li Only \c {QRangeModel} APIs can mutate or read the data, and
            mutation works only for supported types.
    \endlist

    \b {Workaround (for supported types: NumPy arrays, 2D Python arrays):}
    \list
        \li Fetching data (works for all types):
        \code [qml]
        Test_model.data (Test_model.index(row, column))
        \endcode
        \li Inserting rows or columns (NumPy arrays & 2D arrays only):
        \code [qml]
        Test_model.insertRows(row, count)
        Test_model.insertColumns(column, count)
        \endcode
        \li Removing rows or columns (NumPy arrays & 2D arrays only):
        \code [qml]
        Test_model.removeRows(row, count)
        Test_model.removeColumns(column, count)
        \endcode
        \li Updating items (NumPy arrays & 2D arrays only):
        \code [qml]
        Test_model.setData(Test_model.index(row, column), "Updated text")
        \endcode
    \endlist
*/

